### 🔴 Critical #2 — Trivially-True `IsNotNull` Assertions (4 tests)

These tests have an assertion, but it can **never fail** given the types involved:

| Test (line) | Assertion | Why it's trivially true |
|---|---|---|
| `GetProduct_Works` (28) | `Assert.IsNotNull(result)` | Product was just added — will always be found. Never checks SKU/Name/Price. |
| `SearchProducts_Works` (51) | `Assert.IsNotNull(results)` | `List<Product>` from `FindByName` is never null (empty list at worst). |
| `GetAllProducts_Works` (59) | `Assert.IsNotNull(all)` | `List<Product>` from `GetAll()` is never null. |
| `ExportToCsv_Works` (81) | `Assert.IsNotNull(csv)` | `string.Join` never returns null. |

**Fix — assert on content, not just existence:**

```csharp
// GetProduct_Works → verify the returned product's fields
[TestMethod]
public void GetProduct_ExistingSku_ReturnsMatchingProduct()
{
    _catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
    var result = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");

    Assert.IsNotNull(result);
    Assert.AreEqual("SKU-001", result.Sku);
    Assert.AreEqual("Widget", result.Name);
    Assert.AreEqual(9.99m, result.Price);
}

